Revision history for Games-Sokoban-Solver

0.01  (not yet released)
    - First release: the solver pipeline of the Sokoban project of Jiri Broz,
      transplanted into a CPAN distribution.  Engines carried over: Firstbet 3.8
      (ten modes), Festival 0.20 (eight), Fest 1.03 (eight), FirstBack 0.07
      (one), the optimal Solver 3.3 as Games::Sokoban::Solver::Optimal, and the
      level preparation (Code 1.0, Parser 1.0, Festival_Parser 0.02, FestDev
      1.00).  Deadlock patterns are computed at run time by the Finder instead
      of being read from the project's pattern database.
    - Two things the project's engines never met and the Parser now refuses or
      reports instead: a level with a goal no box can ever be pushed onto (the
      Festival engine died on such a level), and a Festival analysis whose two
      budget fuses fired -- the hash carries hotspots_incomplete and
      envelopes_incomplete where the project stopped with an error.
    - The optimal solver keeps its three regimes (DIAL, ASTAR, FIFO) and the
      band; what went was the storage: the database writes, the BerkeleyDB seam
      (the caches live in RAM for one solve), the SQL log and the statistics.
      $LastStatus names the stop, $ON_IMPROVED reports every improvement.
    - The BerkeleyDB branch is optional: Games::Sokoban::Solver::Berkeley (the
      stores), Firstbet::Berk and Optimal::Berk (the twins that keep the search
      on disk) load only where the BerkeleyDB module is installed, under a root
      directory the caller names; a second process on one store directory is
      refused, and the optimal twin refuses a pool over 3 GB (the module keeps
      the size in 32 bits).
    - The race: Games::Sokoban::Solver::Race (the project's Race 0.03) loads the
      thread modules at start() and only for a pool of more than one worker on
      a perl with ithreads; with one worker, or on a perl without them, the same
      submit / next / join calls run every task in this interpreter, one after
      another.  A task may be of another kind than a solve (a handler registered
      at construction), so the deadlock patterns of a level can be dealt out to
      the pool that will race its modes.  Portfolio (the project's Portfolio
      0.01, Tandem 0.02 and the panel of its wave driver): the 27 modes, the
      order of the race, a mode's own limit, the kinds and caps of what runs at
      once, and the one engine face that routes a mode to its engine by name --
      to the BerkeleyDB twin of Firstbet under $BERK.  Config: the settings of
      soko-solver, their defaults, the ini file, and the questions of the
      first run.
    - The facade Games::Sokoban::Solver and the program bin/soko-solver.pl:
      read_levels, pool (one Race pool for a run, the Finder's blok_task as its
      handler), prepare (Level, Code, the Parser with its patterns dealt out to
      the pool in two passes under the budget, the two other analyses -- built
      at once or on demand), first_solution (the race fed by kind under the
      caps, a mode's own limit, the analyses built when the first mode that
      needs them comes up), best_solution (Optimal or its twin, seeded by the
      winner, interruptible through a stop hash), full_path (the forced
      opening in front, the path written again from its pushes, replayed by
      the second reading), solve_level. The program: the settings file and its
      questions on the first run, the four output formats (path, sok, report,
      json), one line per level on standard output and the account on standard
      error, Ctrl+C once for the level in hand and twice for the program, exit
      0 / 1 / 2 / 3. Gates: xt/eq_prepare.pl (prepare from the level's text
      against the project's stored analyses, the pool's patterns against the
      serial ones), xt/cli_roundtrip.pl (the program on levels of the
      collection with threads and without, every path replayed on the level as
      drawn, no proof below a stored one). The round trip found a level the
      race had not solved reported SOLVED in the JSON: fixed before release.
    - The finish: xt/surface.pl audits every module (a sub nobody calls, an
      `our` nobody outside reads, a name of the Sokoban project, a bare exit)
      -- two seam subs went and five knobs became lexical; every module has
      its POD (the two inherited pod files name this distribution's modules
      and say which of their gates and drivers stayed in the project);
      t/16-pod.t; perltidy over the whole tree with the author's profile,
      converged, every gate run again unchanged; maint/prereqs.pl holds
      Makefile.PL to what the modules load; make dist, disttest and an install
      into a prefix of its own pass; .github/workflows/test.yml runs the suite
      and the program on Linux and Windows.
    - Skeleton created with h2xs 1.23 (-AX --skip-exporter --use-new-tests).
